Parameter estimation on the longer sample proves to be extremely challenging for some of the global optimizers. A few give the accurate values, BlackBoxOptim also performs quite well, while others seem to struggle with accuracy a lot.

## First-difference vs. standard loss under noise and bias

This addresses part of [SciMLBenchmarks.jl#28](https://github.com/SciML/SciMLBenchmarks.jl/issues/28):
comparing the *standard* trajectory-matching loss used above (`sum((model - data)^2)`)
against a *first-difference* loss that instead matches consecutive differences
(`sum((diff(model) - diff(data))^2)`). First-differencing is a classic time-series trick for
removing an unknown, roughly-constant *offset* between model and data (a fixed sensor
calibration bias, an unsubtracted baseline level, etc.): differencing exactly cancels an
additive constant, at the cost of amplifying the variance of any random measurement noise
already present (`Var(y_t - y_{t-1})` is twice the noise variance for independent noise).
We reuse the short-horizon problem (`prob_short`, `data_short`) set up above, adding
synthetic measurement noise and, in one scenario, a constant per-channel sensor offset on
top of it.

```julia
Random.seed!(1)
sigma_fd = 0.2
noise_fd = sigma_fd .* randn(size(data_short))
data_short_noisy = data_short .+ noise_fd

sensor_bias = [0.5, -0.3]                        # a fixed, unknown per-channel offset
data_short_biased = data_short .+ noise_fd .+ sensor_bias
```

```julia
function simulate_fd(pvec)
    _prob = remake(prob_short, p = pvec)
    sol = solve(_prob, Tsit5(), saveat = t_short, reltol = 1e-8, abstol = 1e-8)
    sol.retcode == ReturnCode.Success || return nothing
    Array(sol)
end

loss_standard_fd(pvec, data) = (m = simulate_fd(pvec); m === nothing ? 1e10 : sum(abs2, m .- data))
function loss_firstdiff(pvec, data)
    m = simulate_fd(pvec)
    m === nothing && return 1e10
    return sum(abs2, diff(m, dims = 2) .- diff(data, dims = 2))
end

function fit_fd(loss, data; maxiters = 5000)
    optf = OptimizationFunction((pvec, _) -> loss(pvec, data))
    optprob = OptimizationProblem(optf, loc_init, lb = first.(loc_bounds), ub = last.(loc_bounds))
    res = solve(optprob, BBO_adaptive_de_rand_1_bin(); maxiters = maxiters)
    optprob2 = OptimizationProblem(optf, res.u, lb = first.(loc_bounds), ub = last.(loc_bounds))
    solve(optprob2, Opt(:LN_BOBYQA, 4), maxiters = 2000)
end
```

With a pure constant bias and no noise, first-differencing should recover the true
parameters `p` to solver precision (`diff` exactly cancels a per-channel constant added at
every time point), while the standard loss cannot: a constant offset changes the shape of
the trajectory relative to the true dynamics, not just its level, so it isn't reproducible
by adjusting `(a, b, c, d)` alone.

```julia
data_short_bias_only = data_short .+ sensor_bias
res_std_bias = fit_fd(loss_standard_fd, data_short_bias_only)
res_fd_bias = fit_fd(loss_firstdiff, data_short_bias_only)

df_fd_bias = DataFrame(
    loss = ["standard", "first-difference"],
    param_error = [norm(res_std_bias.u .- p), norm(res_fd_bias.u .- p)])
df_fd_bias
```

```julia
bar(["standard", "first-difference"],
    [norm(res_std_bias.u .- p), norm(res_fd_bias.u .- p)],
    yscale = :log10, legend = false, ylabel = "parameter error (log scale)",
    title = "Recovering parameters from a constant bias", size = (600, 400))
```

A single noisy fit is not a reliable comparison on its own; repeat the pure-noise scenario
(no bias) over several random seeds to see which loss is more accurate *on average* when
there is no offset to correct for.

```julia
seeds_range = 1:5
function repeat_seeds_fd(loss; seeds = seeds_range, maxiters = 4000)
    errs = Float64[]
    for s in seeds
        Random.seed!(s)
        noise_s = sigma_fd .* randn(size(data_short))
        res = fit_fd(loss, data_short .+ noise_s; maxiters = maxiters)
        push!(errs, norm(res.u .- p))
    end
    return errs
end

errs_standard_fd = repeat_seeds_fd(loss_standard_fd)
errs_firstdiff_fd = repeat_seeds_fd(loss_firstdiff)

df_fd_seeds = DataFrame(seed = collect(seeds_range),
    standard_err = errs_standard_fd, firstdiff_err = errs_firstdiff_fd)
df_fd_seeds
```

```julia
println("mean standard error:   ", mean(errs_standard_fd))
println("mean firstdiff error:  ", mean(errs_firstdiff_fd))
```

## Single shooting vs. multiple shooting on the long-horizon problem

This addresses another part of [SciMLBenchmarks.jl#28](https://github.com/SciML/SciMLBenchmarks.jl/issues/28):
comparing *single shooting* (integrate the whole time span from one initial condition, as
every fit above does) against *multiple shooting* (split the time span into segments, treat
each segment's own initial state as an additional free parameter, and penalize
discontinuities between consecutive segments). We reuse the long-horizon problem and data
(`prob`, `data`, `time_points`) already set up above -- this is exactly the dataset whose
"extremely challenging" difficulty was already noted, i.e. the regime multiple shooting is
meant to help with. Both methods must estimate `u0` as well as the kinetic parameters
(giving single shooting the exact `u0` for free would understate its real difficulty).

```julia
function simulate_single_ms(pvec, u0vec)
    _prob = remake(prob, u0 = u0vec, p = pvec)
    sol = solve(_prob, Vern9(), saveat = time_points, reltol = 1e-8, abstol = 1e-8, maxiters = 10^6)
    sol.retcode == ReturnCode.Success || return nothing
    Array(sol)
end

function loss_single_ms(theta)
    m = simulate_single_ms(theta[1:4], theta[5:6])
    m === nothing && return 1e10
    return sum(abs2, m .- data)
end

lb_ms_single = vcat(fill(0.1, 4), fill(0.1, 2))
ub_ms_single = vcat(fill(10.0, 4), fill(10.0, 2))
init_ms_single = vcat(fill(5.0, 4), [3.0, 3.0])   # a deliberately poor guess

optf_ms1 = OptimizationFunction((th, _) -> loss_single_ms(th))
optprob_ms1 = OptimizationProblem(optf_ms1, init_ms_single, lb = lb_ms_single, ub = ub_ms_single)
@time res_ms_single = solve(optprob_ms1, BBO_adaptive_de_rand_1_bin(); maxiters = 3000)
res_ms_single.objective
```

Split `[0, 30]` into `K` equal segments. The free parameters are the shared kinetic
parameters plus each segment's own initial state, seeded directly from the data at that
segment's start time -- something single shooting has no equivalent of.

```julia
K = 6
seg_bounds = range(0.0, 30.0, length = K + 1)
seg_idx = [findall(τ -> seg_bounds[k] <= τ <= seg_bounds[k + 1], time_points) for k in 1:K]

function unpack_theta_ms(theta)
    T = eltype(theta)
    pvec = theta[1:4]
    u0s = Vector{Vector{T}}(undef, K)
    for k in 1:K
        u0s[k] = theta[(4 + 2 * (k - 1) + 1):(4 + 2 * (k - 1) + 2)]
    end
    return pvec, u0s
end

function loss_multi_ms(theta; continuity_weight = 100.0)
    pvec, u0s = unpack_theta_ms(theta)
    total = 0.0
    for k in 1:K
        idxs = seg_idx[k]
        _prob = remake(prob, u0 = u0s[k], p = pvec, tspan = (seg_bounds[k], seg_bounds[k + 1]))
        sol = solve(_prob, Vern9(), saveat = time_points[idxs], reltol = 1e-8, abstol = 1e-8,
            maxiters = 10^6)
        sol.retcode == ReturnCode.Success || return 1e10
        total += sum(abs2, Array(sol) .- data[:, idxs])
        if k < K
            total += continuity_weight * sum(abs2, sol.u[end] .- u0s[k + 1])
        end
    end
    return total
end
```

```julia
init_ms_multi = copy(init_ms_single[1:4])
for k in 1:K
    idx0 = argmin(abs.(time_points .- seg_bounds[k]))
    append!(init_ms_multi, data[:, idx0])
end
lb_ms_multi = vcat(lb_ms_single[1:4], fill(0.1, 2 * K))
ub_ms_multi = vcat(ub_ms_single[1:4], fill(10.0, 2 * K))

optf_ms2 = OptimizationFunction((th, _) -> loss_multi_ms(th))
optprob_ms2 = OptimizationProblem(optf_ms2, init_ms_multi, lb = lb_ms_multi, ub = ub_ms_multi)
@time res_ms_multi = solve(optprob_ms2, BBO_adaptive_de_rand_1_bin(); maxiters = 3000 * K)
res_ms_multi.objective
```

```julia
df_ms = DataFrame(
    method = ["Single shooting", "Multiple shooting (K=$K)"],
    param_error = [norm(res_ms_single.u[1:4] .- p), norm(res_ms_multi.u[1:4] .- p)],
    loss = [res_ms_single.objective, res_ms_multi.objective],
    n_free_params = [length(init_ms_single), length(init_ms_multi)])
df_ms
```

Multiple shooting's evaluation budget above is scaled by its parameter count relative to
single shooting's (`3000 * K` vs. `3000`), matching the standard practice of allocating a
global stochastic optimizer's budget roughly in proportion to the dimensionality of the
search space.

```julia
fit_single_ms = simulate_single_ms(res_ms_single.u[1:4], res_ms_single.u[5:6])
p_multi_fit, u0s_fit = unpack_theta_ms(res_ms_multi.u)
fit_multi_ms = similar(data)
for k in 1:K
    idxs = seg_idx[k]
    sol_k = solve(remake(prob, u0 = u0s_fit[k], p = p_multi_fit,
            tspan = (seg_bounds[k], seg_bounds[k + 1])), Vern9(), saveat = time_points[idxs],
        reltol = 1e-8, abstol = 1e-8)
    fit_multi_ms[:, idxs] = Array(sol_k)
end

plot(time_points, data[1, :], label = "data (x)", color = :black, linewidth = 2,
    xlabel = "time", title = "Fitted trajectories vs. data", legend = :outertopright,
    size = (900, 450))
plot!(time_points, data[2, :], label = "data (y)", color = :gray, linewidth = 2)
plot!(time_points, fit_single_ms[1, :], label = "single shooting (x)", linestyle = :dash)
plot!(time_points, fit_single_ms[2, :], label = "single shooting (y)", linestyle = :dash)
plot!(time_points, fit_multi_ms[1, :], label = "multiple shooting (x)", linestyle = :dot)
plot!(time_points, fit_multi_ms[2, :], label = "multiple shooting (y)", linestyle = :dot)
vline!(collect(seg_bounds), label = "segment boundaries", color = :lightgray, alpha = 0.5)
```

A single race can be won or lost by chance; repeat both fits over a handful of seeds
(`BlackBoxOptim`'s population initialization is itself randomized) to see whether either
method is more *reliable*, not just luckier on one run.

```julia
function run_seeds_ms(loss, init, lb, ub, budget, seeds)
    errs = Float64[]
    for s in seeds
        Random.seed!(s)
        optf = OptimizationFunction((th, _) -> loss(th))
        optprob = OptimizationProblem(optf, init, lb = lb, ub = ub)
        res = solve(optprob, BBO_adaptive_de_rand_1_bin(); maxiters = budget)
        push!(errs, norm(res.u[1:4] .- p))
    end
    return errs
end

seeds_ms = 1:3
errs_single_ms = run_seeds_ms(loss_single_ms, init_ms_single, lb_ms_single, ub_ms_single, 3000, seeds_ms)
errs_multi_ms = run_seeds_ms(loss_multi_ms, init_ms_multi, lb_ms_multi, ub_ms_multi, 3000 * K, seeds_ms)

bar(["single shooting", "multiple shooting"], [mean(errs_single_ms), mean(errs_multi_ms)],
    yerror = [std(errs_single_ms), std(errs_multi_ms)], legend = false,
    ylabel = "parameter error (mean +/- std over seeds)",
    title = "Reliability across $(length(seeds_ms)) random seeds", size = (500, 400))
```

# Conclusion

In general we observe that lower tolerances lead to higher accuracy but too low tolerance could affect the convergence time drastically. Also fitting a shorter timespan seems to be easier in comparison (quite intuitively). NLopt methods seem to give great accuracy in the shorter problem with a lot of the algorithms giving 0 fitness, BBO performs very well on it with marginal change with `tol` values. In the case of global optimization for the longer problem, there is some difference in performance among the algorithms with `LD_SLSQP` `GN_ESCH` `GN_ISRES` `GN_ORIG_DIRECT_L` performing among the worst, BBO also gives a bit high fitness in comparison.

The two extra comparisons above answer [issue #28](https://github.com/SciML/SciMLBenchmarks.jl/issues/28)'s
loss-function and shooting-formulation questions on this same model and data. The two
losses trade off exactly as the noise-amplification argument predicts: with pure,
zero-mean measurement noise and no offset, the standard loss gives a lower average
parameter error, since first-differencing doubles the effective noise variance without
buying anything in return; with a constant, unknown additive bias, first-differencing
recovers the true parameters almost exactly while the standard loss converges to a
measurably biased answer. On the long-horizon shooting comparison, single shooting is
consistently *more* accurate than this multiple-shooting formulation here, even with its
evaluation budget scaled up to match its larger parameter count -- a useful, if unglamorous,
finding, since this bounded, non-chaotic oscillation doesn't put single-shooting integration
itself into the numerically unstable regime where multiple shooting's classical advantage
(short segments insulated from a bad guess blowing up over a long integration) would show up.

